101: THE MATERIAL YOU ARE GIVEN
102: One zip, `S6_06_BLIND_background.zip`; `MANIFEST.tsv` lists every file with its origin and the reason it is attached.
103: - **[AC26]**: Claude and L. Alpöge, *The (3,4,∞) modular family of 2-tori, completed at its three special points, is a complex structure on S6* (2026, https://alpo.ge/s6.pdf): `original/AC26_original_manuscript.pdf` (the authority; a PDF because it has no LaTeX source) and `original/AC26_transcription.tex` (a LaTeX transcription made by AI from the PDF; where a formula looks wrong, look at the PDF and say so). Its page 2 has the Setup with the period laws; its Section 7 the twist data and the fundamental group.
104: - **[E26]**: P. Engel, *Complex structures on S6*, arXiv:2609.38442, `papers/arXiv_2609.38442_Engel_src/` (LaTeX source): the elliptic surface with fibres IV*, III, I₁, the section P of height 1/6, the line bundle M = O(P − O), the linearization, the logarithmic transformations, Remarks 8.1-8.5 and Conjecture 8.2.
105: - **The other families** (`papers/`, LaTeX sources): J. Viaclovsky, arXiv:2610.39102, arXiv:2609.33785, arXiv:2610.10451; W. Liu and S. Rollenske, arXiv:2609.33826; X. Yang, arXiv:2609.29666; Z. Wang, arXiv:2609.11541.
106: - **The data** (`data/`): `fourfold_tuple.py` (the matrices of Part 1; data only) and `stated_values.csv` (the earlier runs' rows for the items of this prompt: run, id, statement, status; AI-produced by GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT Pro mode; read once or not at all by anyone else; no proofs).
107: Nothing else is attached, by design: no proof text, no review, and no paper that states the answers of Part 5.
108: 
109: CONVENTIONS (fix them on your first page and keep them)
110: - B = P¹ with coordinate t; p₁ = {t = 0}, p₂ = {t = 1}, p₀ = {t = ∞}; multiplicities m₁ = 3, m₂ = 4; f: X → B; S_j the reduced multiple fibres, W = f⁻¹(p₀) the cusp fibre.
111: - Λ = Z⁴ with basis (γ̂, û, ŵ, δ̂) is H₁ of a regular fibre; monodromies A₁, A₂, M₀ as in [AC26]; period matrix Π_c; D = Im β − 6(Im μ)²/Im τ; w = e^{−πic}; the family exists exactly for |w| < 216.
112: - For Part 1 keep the two conventions apart: A, B act on cohomology, M_i = their transposes on homology.
113: - State on your first page the dictionary between [AC26] and [E26] that you use, with signs and orientations of meridians, and keep it.
114: 
115: RULES OF PROOF (they bind every statement you deliver)
116: 1. State the actual claim with all its data: which manifold, which family and which twist data, which fibre (the reduced surface or the full fibre), which coefficients (Z, Q, C), which orientation and sign conventions. Do not replace it by a convenient special case; if you prove a special case, name it as one; the general statement stays on your list as an item you are still working on.
117: 2. Type every map: source, target, the category in which it is a morphism (holomorphic, real-analytic, continuous; of sheaves, of lattices, of spaces over the base), and each property you use.
118: 3. Every reduction ("after base change", "on the normalization", "on the cyclic cover", "restricting to a fibre", "we may assume") comes with its comparison map and a proof that the exact property needed transfers in the direction needed.
119: 4. "Up to" names the ambiguity. Keep apart: biholomorphic and diffeomorphic; oriented and unoriented; isotopic and homotopic; integral and rational statements; a class and a representative.
120: 5. Check the special places: each special fibre; the strata of the cusp fibres; the non-reduced structure of the multiple fibres; torsion in homology; the corner (t, r) = (∞, 0); the ends of each parameter domain.
121: 6. A counterexample is given completely and, when finite or computable, checked by a program whose code and output you attach. One verified counterexample refutes the statement: say "false", and then find the strongest true statement in its place and prove it. An invalid proof alone shows a gap, not falsity; keep the two apart.
122: 7. No authority. The stated values, the leads, the earlier runs' rows and the assertions of [AC26] beyond its construction are all to be proved or refuted by you. The papers with human authors may be used with exact theorem numbers, after you have read the statement you use; if you find an error in one, say so and show it.
123: 8. Never invent a reference; mark each entry of the bibliography `verified` (you opened it) or `unverified`. For every item, establish its literature status as of the day of the run, within the rules of blindness: cite every result that proves, refutes, sharpens or bears on it, compare its statement with yours, and say whether yours is new, a new proof of a known statement, or independent evidence for a conclusion already in the literature.
124: 9. What is not yet settled stays on the page as an exact statement, with what you tried and the step that fails, and you go on attacking it; it never finishes an item. Never write it up as proved, and never drop it.
125: 10. Known and Open are not end points. A Known statement is derived from the cited theorem in the form needed here, with the proof of the reduction. A statement is labelled Open only in a return marked INCOMPLETE (point 4 of the outcome section); it then records your own attack: the strongest partial result you proved, the computations you ran, the obstruction as an exact statement, every approach tried with where it failed. A row "open, see [reference]" is never acceptable.
126: 11. A number obtained numerically is given with its precision and computed by two independent methods; it supports a statement and does not prove it, unless it comes with a rigorous error bound (interval arithmetic), in which case say so. Exact integer and rational computations are done exactly, by program, with the code attached.
127: 
128: WHAT TO DELIVER
129: One ZIP containing:
130: - `<ID>_paper.tex` and its PDF (LuaLaTeX, compiles cleanly): the mathematics in the order of the Parts, every statement numbered and labelled Proved / Refuted / Known (with citation and derivation), with complete proofs (the label Open exists only in a return marked INCOMPLETE). English, first person plural, the register of a careful research paper. No bullet lists where an argument belongs, no summaries of what you are about to do, no closing recapitulations.
131: - `<ID>_results.csv`: one row per numbered statement: `id, statement, status, where_proved, depends_on, item_of_the_prompt, stated_value_compared (run and id), agrees_with_stated_value, notes`.
132: - `<ID>_references.bib`.
133: - `<ID>_literature_log.md`: every source opened, in order, with the time, the reason and what was taken from it; for Part 5, the checkpoint that precedes the first opening of arXiv:2610.00250.
134: - `<ID>_report.md`: the two tables of point 1; what you read, what you computed; every disagreement with a stated value, with your value and its proof; every correction to [AC26], [E26] and the other papers; and, only in a return marked INCOMPLETE, the undecided items with the state reached, every approach tried and the next step.
135: - `code/`: every program you ran, with its output.
136: The work is AI-produced by GPT-6 Astra (OpenAI, ChatGPT Pro mode); credit it so, attribute nothing to any person, and claim no priority: where a result is known, cite it; where you did not find it, say what you searched.
137: 
138: This task is long by design. Work through all Parts completely; do not stop at an outline, a sample or a plan, and do not ask whether to continue. Take the time the work needs. Write the files from the start and extend them as you go, so that an interruption loses little.
139: 
140: IF THE EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT FAILS
141: If the workspace disconnects or the attached zip cannot be opened, wait and retry for ten minutes. If it does not come back, write the work out in the chat as text: every deliverable file in full, each in its own code block headed by its file name, taken from the code that saved it; then continue the mathematics in the chat until it is done. An answer that only reports the failure or asks for the files again is not acceptable.
142: 
143: **Typesetting check.** Every PDF you deliver is published as it is. Before you deliver one, read its build log: no overfull box may stick out by more than 2pt, whether a diagram, a display or a line of text. Fix each one in the source (scale or split a wide diagram, break a wide display, let a line break) and compile again. Then render pages to images and look at them: every page that had an overflow, and a sample spread over the document. Give the counts before and after in the report.
144: 
145: **Checkpoints.** Save a checkpoint ZIP to the Library once every hour of work, each under a new name, `<ID>_checkpoint_<NN>.zip` with `NN` counting up; never overwrite, rename or delete an earlier checkpoint. If the workspace comes back empty, restore it from the newest checkpoint and from this conversation before you continue. The final ZIP holds everything the checkpoints held: a file or passage left out of the final is listed in the report with the reason (superseded by which file, or wrong).
146: Save checkpoints once an hour and at no other time: none after a page, part, section, cote, folder, chapter or reader. A checkpoint is
147: one ZIP holding every deliverable written so far and everything that documents the work (records, notes, logs,
148: scripts, the report so far), so that a new session can continue from it and the files attached to this prompt. Leave
149: the attached files themselves out of it, the scans and sources you were given: we have them. It is never a JSON file,
150: a recovery file, a summary, or an export of the newest pages only. The checkpoints are what keeps the work if this
151: session dies: work that is in no checkpoint is lost.
